---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { KpisFrameworkDiagram } from "../../components/react";
---

<Layout
  role="visitor"
  breadcrumbs={[
    { label: "Home", href: "/" },
    { label: "Methods & quality" },
    { label: "Evaluation framework" },
  ]}
  backHref="/"
>
  <div class="mx-auto px-4 py-8 max-w-4xl prose prose-gray">
    <h1>Evaluation Framework (SIEF)</h1>

    <div class="not-prose">
      <KpisFrameworkDiagram />
    </div>

    <section>
      <h2>What SIEF is and why it exists</h2>
      <p>
        The SUM Integrated Evaluation Framework (SIEF) provides a structured methodology for
        measuring the impact of New Shared Mobility (NSM) services on Public Transport integration
        across European cities. It was developed within the Horizon Europe SUM project to ensure
        that data collected across different Living Labs remains comparable and scientifically rigorous.
      </p>
      <p>
        SIEF defines which Key Performance Indicators (KPIs) are collected, how they are calculated,
        and what evaluation criteria are applied. It draws on established frameworks from sustainable
        urban mobility planning (SUMP) and transport research, adapted for the specific context of
        shared mobility integration.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>The KPI framework</h2>
      <p>
        The framework organises KPIs into four evaluation domains:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Accessibility</strong> — how well shared mobility services reach different population groups and areas</li>
        <li><strong>Safety</strong> — incident rates, perceived safety, and infrastructure conditions</li>
        <li><strong>Environmental impact</strong> — modal shift toward sustainable transport, emissions reduction</li>
        <li><strong>Economic viability</strong> — cost efficiency, service adoption rates, and business sustainability</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Each KPI has a defined calculation methodology, data requirements, and target direction
        (whether higher or lower values indicate improvement). Full specifications are available
        in the KPI Framework PDF.
      </p>
      <p>
        <a href="/files/SIEF_All_KPIs-template.xlsx" class="text-primary hover:underline">
          Download KPI Framework spreadsheet →
        </a>
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Evaluation criteria and methodology</h2>
      <p>
        SIEF uses a before/after measurement approach: cities collect baseline KPI values before
        implementing mobility measures, and then collect post-implementation values to assess impact.
        This enables the platform to quantify changes attributable to specific measures using
        ridge regression analysis.
      </p>
      <p>
        The evaluation covers both quantitative metrics (measurable values with units) and
        qualitative assessments (survey-based perceptions). Together they provide a comprehensive
        picture of how NSM integration affects urban mobility patterns.
      </p>
    </section>

    <nav class="not-prose mt-8 flex gap-4 text-sm">
      <a href="/methods/collection-plan" class="text-primary hover:underline">Next: Data collection →</a>
      <a href="/methods/glossary" class="text-primary hover:underline">Glossary →</a>
    </nav>
  </div>
</Layout>
